WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.040
$20 bought only three minutes from New York to San Francisco before loading coils arrived. This is First Principles Daily, Episode 131. John J. Cardi's team installed vacuum tube repeaters in 1915 and carried a commercial call across the continent for the first time. Michael Pupin had patented loading coils in December 1899. George Campbell, working in parallel at AT&amp;T, had already shown the same electrical effect on laboratory lines.

00:00:29.040 --> 00:00:29.160
designs.

00:00:29.500 --> 00:00:34.620
Before inductive loading, capacitance bled a long circuit so badly that engineers faced

00:00:34.620 --> 00:00:35.400
a hard choice.

00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:38.960
Keep the spanned short or pay for thick copper the whole way.

00:00:39.720 --> 00:00:44.540
Coils placed at regular intervals let one pair of wires carry intelligible speech hundreds

00:00:44.540 --> 00:00:48.080
of miles farther, without a matching rise in metal or power.

00:00:49.071 --> 00:00:53.911
The repeaters then took that quieter line and added active gain, so the same principle

00:00:53.911 --> 00:00:55.011
could cross the country.

00:00:55.011 --> 00:01:01.331
Telephone engineers in the 1880s and early 1890s treated distance as a scaling problem.

00:01:01.691 --> 00:01:06.511
More wire, heavier wire, or another human operator sooner along the route.

00:01:07.111 --> 00:01:10.911
New York to Chicago Service opened in 1892 on that plan.

00:01:11.491 --> 00:01:16.991
Chicago Public Library records show the connection stayed costly and unreliable for decades afterward.

00:01:17.591 --> 00:01:22.991
Each added mile raised both resistance and capacitance, so signal strength fell exponentially.

00:01:22.991 --> 00:01:27.751
eventually. The only remedies anyone knew were to lay still more copper, or to accept

00:01:27.751 --> 00:01:32.311
that the voice would fade. Companies priced the call to recover the capital locked in

00:01:32.311 --> 00:01:36.771
that copper, plus the labor of switchboard operators who patched every long distance

00:01:36.771 --> 00:01:42.611
circuit by hand. The design assumed attenuation was an immutable property of the wire, not

00:01:42.611 --> 00:01:47.391
a quantity you could tune by adding a different electrical element. So the finished price stayed

00:01:47.391 --> 00:01:52.451
high because the plant itself stayed large and specialized. Every extra mile demanded

00:01:52.451 --> 00:01:57.731
its own poles, cross arms, and insulators. The copper had to be drawn thicker so total

00:01:57.731 --> 00:02:03.311
resistance stayed low enough for voice frequencies to survive the trip. Operator labor at each

00:02:03.311 --> 00:02:08.511
manual board was another fixed cost per call. You could not spread that labor across greater

00:02:08.511 --> 00:02:13.171
distance without raising the rate. Because the design copied the short haul model and

00:02:13.171 --> 00:02:17.771
simply stretched it, nobody asked whether the loss itself could be changed at the electrical

00:02:17.771 --> 00:02:20.451
level instead of buried under brute mass of metal.

00:02:21.071 --> 00:02:23.911
Capital intensity grew roughly in line with root length.

00:02:24.471 --> 00:02:27.891
The permanent charge stayed pinned to the full weight of that infrastructure.

00:02:28.949 --> 00:02:33.729
Pupin and Campbell started from the transmission line equations, not from the plant already

00:02:33.729 --> 00:02:34.329
in the ground.

00:02:34.989 --> 00:02:39.589
A telephone wire is a distributed network of resistance, inductance and capacitance.

00:02:39.589 --> 00:02:45.409
accidents. Inductance and capacitance together make the loss depend on frequency. Voice is

00:02:45.409 --> 00:02:50.649
not one tone, it is a band. A line that treats the top of that band worse than the bottom

00:02:50.649 --> 00:02:56.249
does not merely get quieter. It stops sounding like speech. Adding discrete inductance at

00:02:56.249 --> 00:03:01.449
intervals cancels much of the capacitive reactance. The attenuation curve flattens across

00:03:01.449 --> 00:03:07.029
the voice frequencies. Picture the line as a long chain of tiny leaks. Capacidences

00:03:07.029 --> 00:03:09.449
is the leak that grows worse as the tone rises.

00:03:10.129 --> 00:03:12.369
A coil does not plug the leak with more metal.

00:03:12.849 --> 00:03:18.029
It answers the leak with the opposite electrical behavior, spaced so the answer arrives before

00:03:18.029 --> 00:03:19.329
the speech has already died.

00:03:20.009 --> 00:03:25.309
The magic wand floor is the market price of drawn copper, plus the modest cost of winding

00:03:25.309 --> 00:03:26.769
coils from that same metal.

00:03:27.469 --> 00:03:31.969
Wave the wand, arrange the atoms, and you are not buying poles, huts, or operators.

00:03:32.609 --> 00:03:33.949
You are buying metal, shaped.

00:03:34.649 --> 00:03:42.469
Contemporary accounts of the 1915 Transcontinental Line list roughly 2,960 tons of copper for the whole route.

00:03:42.909 --> 00:03:48.749
At the copper prices then prevailing, that quantity was a small fraction of the capital already tied up in

00:03:48.749 --> 00:03:55.089
poles, insulators, and terminal equipment. Walk that comparison. The metal in the circuit was the

00:03:55.089 --> 00:04:00.929
theoretical floor. The finished plant cost many times that floor, because most of the money sat in

00:04:00.929 --> 00:04:04.469
everything required to make an untuned wire usable over distance.

00:04:05.189 --> 00:04:06.929
That gap is the idiot index.

00:04:07.509 --> 00:04:10.909
On the preloading design, the index was high for a specific reason.

00:04:11.489 --> 00:04:16.649
The expense lived in the sheer volume of copper needed to overpower loss, not in any rare

00:04:16.649 --> 00:04:18.349
value inside the metal itself.

00:04:19.009 --> 00:04:20.009
Copper was ordinary.

00:04:20.669 --> 00:04:24.009
The quantity required to bully a bad circuit into working was not.

00:04:24.669 --> 00:04:29.469
Once loading coils sat every few miles, the same pair could span far greater distances

00:04:29.469 --> 00:04:35.449
with acceptable loss, required copper mass per circuit mile fell, Campbell showed the effect

00:04:35.449 --> 00:04:42.689
first on laboratory lines. Pupins December 1899 patent gave AT&amp;T clear rights and the company

00:04:42.689 --> 00:04:47.249
bought those rights so it could control the improvement, making the coils practical meant

00:04:47.249 --> 00:04:52.389
holding inductance steady against temperature and handling. That demanded standardized winding

00:04:52.389 --> 00:04:57.849
machines and core materials that could be made in quantity. Each coil added a small series

00:04:57.849 --> 00:05:03.709
resistance of its own. The trade was still favorable. Thinner wire over longer spans cut total

00:05:03.709 --> 00:05:09.689
copper by more than the coils themselves added back. The next decisive step came in 1915.

00:05:10.349 --> 00:05:15.989
Arnold's vacuum tube repeaters installed under Cardi's direction supplied active gain at intervals

00:05:15.989 --> 00:05:21.649
instead of relying only on passive compensation. Each repeater station replaced hundreds of miles

00:05:21.649 --> 00:05:25.769
of additional copper that would otherwise have been needed to keep the signal above the noise.

00:05:25.769 --> 00:05:31.269
days. The redesign hit two cost drivers in sequence. First, the passive loss mechanism,

00:05:31.849 --> 00:05:36.789
then the need for continuous metal to fight that loss. Later repeater stations brought

00:05:36.789 --> 00:05:41.589
their own maintenance. The arithmetic still favored the change. One active amplifier

00:05:41.589 --> 00:05:47.569
every 50 to 100 miles stood in for many additional tons of copper and thousands of extra poles

00:05:47.569 --> 00:05:53.708
that passive compensation alone would have demanded. The 1915 line made commercial New York

00:05:53.708 --> 00:05:59.808
to San Francisco's service possible. The New York Times reported the opening price at $20.70

00:05:59.808 --> 00:06:05.228
for the first three minutes. Coils and repeaters had lowered the copper tonnage per usable circuit.

00:06:05.868 --> 00:06:10.648
Poles, real estate for repeater huts, and manual switchboard labor still held the per-minute

00:06:10.648 --> 00:06:16.708
charge far above the raw material floor. Chicago Public Library sources note that long distance

00:06:16.708 --> 00:06:23.028
calls state expensive and inconvenient until well after World War II. The idiot index had come down.

00:06:23.028 --> 00:06:26.888
It had not yet reached the physical limits set by copper resistance alone.

00:06:27.668 --> 00:06:34.448
Each new layer, coils, tubes, and later carrier multiplexing, still needed its own infrastructure and its own maintenance.

00:06:35.108 --> 00:06:42.888
Even after the coils cut copper mass per mile, the system needed one physical circuit pair per conversation until multiplexing arrived.

00:06:43.588 --> 00:06:47.408
Wire and poles therefore stayed large relative to the value of the raw metal.

00:06:47.408 --> 00:06:53.308
Repeater huts added land, power, and skilled technicians at intervals across 13 states.

00:06:53.528 --> 00:06:57.968
Those costs could not come out until solid state electronics and automated switching

00:06:57.968 --> 00:07:02.648
reduced both the number of intermediate points and the people required to staff them.

00:07:03.568 --> 00:07:07.908
A circuit whose cost is dominated by the metal needed to overcome its own electrical

00:07:07.908 --> 00:07:11.088
shortcomings is telling you where the bottleneck actually sits.

00:07:11.668 --> 00:07:14.868
It is the circuit's frequency response, not the price of copper.

00:07:14.868 --> 00:07:20.308
The old plant kept buying thickness because thickness was the only knob the short haul model had

00:07:20.308 --> 00:07:26.328
taught anyone to turn. Pupin and Campbell turned a different knob. They treated attenuation as a

00:07:26.328 --> 00:07:31.968
tunable parameter, not a fixed tax on distance. That is what let the coils shrink the plant before

00:07:31.968 --> 00:07:38.244
any later electronics arrived. The magic wand makes the distinction visible. If the finished thing

00:07:38.244 --> 00:07:43.584
costs many times the arranged atoms, you are paying for a process that has not yet been questioned.

00:07:43.584 --> 00:07:48.844
Here, the unquestioned process was letting capacitance set the gauge of the wire, then

00:07:48.844 --> 00:07:52.024
letting that gauge set the poles, the labor, and the tariff.

00:07:52.524 --> 00:07:56.964
Insert a compensating element at the right physical scale, and the downstream plant can

00:07:56.964 --> 00:07:57.724
shrink with it.

00:07:58.582 --> 00:08:03.302
The coils had to be close enough, and consistent enough, that the correction met the loss

00:08:03.302 --> 00:08:04.902
where the loss was accumulating.

00:08:05.502 --> 00:08:09.422
A brilliant part in the wrong place would have left the exponential fade untouched.

00:08:09.422 --> 00:08:14.962
The repeaters show the same discipline one step later, active gain belonged at intervals

00:08:14.962 --> 00:08:18.782
of tens of miles, not at every pole, and not only at the two ends.

00:08:19.783 --> 00:08:24.263
Put the correction at the scale of the problem, and copper, poles, and people stop having

00:08:24.263 --> 00:08:26.423
to impersonate a missing electrical function.

00:08:27.043 --> 00:08:31.723
The same move reappears whenever a system's losses are treated as design variables instead

00:08:31.723 --> 00:08:32.323
of constants.

00:08:33.103 --> 00:08:37.683
Who will next locate the equivalent compensating element inside today's data center power

00:08:37.683 --> 00:08:39.503
chains or long-haul freight networks.

00:08:40.063 --> 00:08:42.543
That's a wrap on today's first principles breakdown.

00:08:43.163 --> 00:08:45.583
The ideas travel further when you pass them on.

00:08:46.063 --> 00:08:47.383
Send this to a fellow builder.

00:08:47.943 --> 00:08:49.063
I'm Patrick and Vancouver.

